    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i checked the fluid and it actually looks pretty clean, no burnt smell or anything. i'm leaning towards it being something in the valve body or maybe a solenoid acting up. if i pull the pan to look at the solenoids, is there anything specific i should be looking for besides just visual debris? like, are there certain patterns in the pressure spikes that would point me more toward a mechanical valve issue vs an electrical sensor issue?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When inspecting the solenoids, look for signs of wear or lubricant degradation, such as dry contacts, corrosion, or oxidized seals. Patterns in the pressure spikes can also provide a clue, as significant spikes tend to occur during sudden transitions between gear ratios. Mechanical issues like worn or machined seals might give you some unusual patterns, such as staggering or random spike positions. However, electrical sensor issues might just cause steady, continuous spikes.",
      "source": "target",
